// GENERATED by aganitha-ie-skill-creator. Never hand-edit.
// No generator block here — generators.baml carries it. Two generators sharing
// a name makes BAML refuse to build ("cannot be re-defined").

// A client with no retry policy cannot honour a 429 at all, which
// [[respect-source-rate-limits]] requires: back off, do not retry tighter.
retry_policy KolExponential {
  max_retries 3
  strategy {
    type exponential_backoff
    delay_ms 300
    multiplier 1.5
    max_delay_ms 10000
  }
}

// Reuses the project's existing client contract: provider google-ai, model
// gemini-3-flash-preview, api_key env.GEMINI_API_KEY — the same client
// SummarizeSection already uses. Declared here under its own name so this skill
// does not edit the project's clients.baml.
client<llm> KolGemini {
  provider google-ai
  retry_policy KolExponential
  options {
    model "gemini-3-flash-preview"
    api_key env.GEMINI_API_KEY
    // Sized from the largest expected record: one long grand-rounds transcript
    // can yield several KOLs each with a multi-sentence opinion. A truncated
    // extraction is indistinguishable from a short video.
    max_tokens 8192
  }
}

// `guidelines` is passed in rather than baked into the prompt, so
// references/guidelines.md stays the single source of the practice.
function ExtractKolVideo(
  search_name: string,
  video_metadata_json: string,
  transcript: string,
  candidate_experts_json: string,
  guidelines: string,
  run_context: string,
  chunk_label: string,
  prior_extraction_json: string
) -> KolVideoExtraction {
  client KolGemini
  prompt #"
    You are an expert assistant specialized in analyzing YouTube video content
    related to healthcare, focusing on identifying and categorizing content
    discussing the disease '{{ search_name }}'.

    Given the transcript and metadata of one YouTube video, analyze BOTH the
    transcript and the metadata to determine the video type and extract
    structured information according to the following rules.

    Follow these guidelines exactly. Where a guideline and your judgement
    disagree, the guideline wins and you record the deviation in assumptions.
    {{ guidelines }}

    Run context (tools available, whether a person can be asked):
    {{ run_context }}

    Video metadata (JSON — video_id, title, description, channel_name,
    published_date, view_count, duration_seconds):
    {{ video_metadata_json }}

    Identity-resolved candidate experts for this disease, each carrying the
    canonical name spelling, affiliation and upstream evidence:
    {{ candidate_experts_json }}

    Transcript segment {{ chunk_label }} of this video. A long talk is split
    into ordered segments; you are seeing one of them, not the whole video:
    {{ transcript }}

    Extraction accumulated from EARLIER segments of this same video (empty on the
    first segment):
    {{ prior_extraction_json }}

    WORKING ACROSS SEGMENTS
    - Treat prior_extraction_json as established. Do not contradict it, do not
      repeat it back verbatim, and do not drop a KOL it already found merely
      because this segment does not mention them.
    - When this segment adds to a stance already recorded for a person, return
      that person with the opinion EXTENDED — the fuller argument, incorporating
      what came before, not a fragment about this segment alone.
    - When this segment introduces a person not yet seen, add them.
    - Classification is a property of the whole video. If an earlier segment
      established video_type, keep it; only revise when this segment contains
      clear evidence the earlier reading was wrong, and log an assumption saying
      what changed it.
    - A speaker introduction usually sits in the first segment and the substance
      later. Absence of a stance in one segment is not evidence of no stance.

    INSTRUCTIONS
    - Confirm the content (transcript or metadata) discusses '{{ search_name }}'
      or related terms — common synonyms, subtypes, or associated medical terms.
      If it is not discussed, set discusses_disease false and return an EMPTY
      records array.
    - Determine the video type:
      - "KOL interview": content featuring healthcare professionals (doctors,
        researchers) discussing '{{ search_name }}'. Look for professional
        titles ("Dr.", "MD"), medical credentials, or expertise mentions in the
        transcript, title, description or channel_name.
      - "patient story": personal accounts of living with '{{ search_name }}' —
        symptoms, diagnosis experience, patient perspective — without a KOL.
      - "informational": general information, news or educational material about
        '{{ search_name }}' without focusing on a KOL or a patient story.
    - If the video is NOT a KOL interview, return an EMPTY records array. This
      function is for KOL analysis only. Always return the run record regardless.
    - Emit one record per KOL identified. If multiple KOLs appear, emit a
      separate record for each.

    KOL NAME AND EXPERTISE
    - Extract the KOL's name from the transcript, description or title,
      prioritising explicit mentions.
    - Split into firstname and lastname. If only one name is given, assign it to
      firstname and set lastname null.
    - kol_name: include the "Dr." prefix ONLY if explicitly present. Remove all
      other prefixes — "Mr.", "Ms.", "Miss" — and roles such as "Professor" or
      "Associate Professor", keeping only the name.
    - If expertise is attached to the name ("Neurology Lead", "Oncology
      Specialist"), strip it from the name into expertise ("Neurology",
      "Oncology"). Remove parentheses and other qualifiers.
      - "Professor Ameen Noushad, Neurology Lead" -> kol_name "Ameen Noushad",
        firstname "Ameen", lastname "Noushad", expertise "Neurology"
      - "Dr. Taylor Hayden, MD" -> kol_name "Dr. Taylor Hayden",
        firstname "Taylor", lastname "Hayden", expertise null
      - "Ms. Elizabeth Green" -> kol_name "Elizabeth Green", expertise null
      - "Dr. John" -> kol_name "Dr. John", firstname "John", lastname null
    - PREFER the canonical spelling from candidate_experts_json over any name
      heard in the transcript: auto-captions mistranscribe names constantly
      ("Eric Kazarian" for Kezirian, "Raj Dia" for Dedhia). Apply the prefix and
      role rules above to that canonical spelling.
    - Match a transcript speaker to a candidate on given AND family name, never
      family name alone. Never merge two people on a matching surname; never
      split one person over a differing affiliation.

    FIELD RULES
    - affiliation: "Institution, Country" (e.g. "UCSF, USA") from the transcript
      or description, else null. Prefer the candidate record's affiliation over
      one spoken aloud; where they genuinely differ, emit the candidate's and
      log a conflict carrying both readings.
    THREE SEPARATE FIELDS REPLACE A SINGLE 'OPINION'. Keeping them apart is the
    point: a factual recap of practice and a position the speaker takes are
    different things, and collapsing them makes descriptive teaching content read
    as advocacy.

    - current_practice: DESCRIPTIVE, no judgement required. What treatments or
      management approaches this speaker says are being used now — standard of
      care, sequencing, patient selection, diagnostic pathway. A factual recap of
      practice as they report it. Null if none is described.

    - stance: THE ACTUAL OPINION. An explicit position, disagreement, criticism,
      or call to change practice: 'X is overused', 'the guidelines are behind the
      evidence', 'Y should be first-line', 'that trial was underpowered', 'this
      is not an appropriate treatment'. It must be a position the speaker TAKES,
      not a fact they report. NULL if the video is purely descriptive. Much
      teaching and explanatory content carries no stance at all, and null is then
      the correct answer, not a failure — do not manufacture a position from a
      neutral explanation.

    - unmet_needs: DESCRIPTIVE, not opinion. Pain points and gaps the speaker
      EXPLICITLY names — side effects, access or cost barriers, diagnostic delay,
      non-adherence, absence of durable response, populations being missed. Only
      what is stated. Do not infer a gap from the subject matter.

    None of the three may restate the title, summarise the channel, or import
    views from the speaker's publications.
    - video_url: "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=" plus the video_id.
    - Do NOT populate publications. It is derived downstream from firstname and
      the first word of lastname, and is set null here.
    - Copy video_id, title, channel_name, view_count, published_date and
      duration_seconds verbatim from the metadata. Do not restyle or round them.
    - published_date must be YYYY-MM-DD.
    - Every field is a string or null. Leave a field null rather than inventing
      it: an empty field is a fact, a fabricated one is a defect.

    Record what was examined, not only what was found — the denominator is what
    makes an absence readable. Set transcript_available false when no captions
    were supplied, and do not let that become an empty finding about the video.

    {{ ctx.output_format }}
  "#
}
